WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:11.500
<v Dana>Morning everyone. Thanks for making the time. I'm recording this one so nobody has to take notes — shout if you'd rather I didn't.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.000
<v Priya>Fine by me.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:31.000
<v Dana>Great. Three things today: the pilot rollout, the pricing change, and then the support backlog. Let's start with the pilot rollout.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:48.000
<v Marcus>So we've had eleven of the fifteen pilot customers onboarded. The four that are stuck are all waiting on the SSO integration, which is the same blocker we had last month.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:01:02.000
<v Priya>It's not the same blocker, actually. Two of them are on Okta and that works now. The other two are on a self-hosted Keycloak and we never tested that path.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:14.000
<v Dana>How long to test it?

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:29.000
<v Priya>I'll spin up a Keycloak instance and run the integration suite against it. Give me until Thursday.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:41.000
<v Marcus>If it works, can we get the two remaining customers onboarded before the end of the month?

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:55.000
<v Priya>Should be fine. The onboarding itself is half a day each once auth is sorted.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:12.000
<v Dana>Okay, so we're going with the Keycloak test first and we'll decide about the deadline once Priya reports back. Marcus, can you let those two customers know we're on it?

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:20.000
<v Marcus>Yep, I'll email them today.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:34.000
<v Dana>Right, moving on to pricing. Sam, this is yours.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:56.000
<v Sam>Thanks. So the proposal is to move the mid tier from forty-nine to sixty-five a seat, and to fold the analytics add-on into it rather than charging separately. Finance modelled it at about eleven percent revenue uplift on the existing base, assuming five percent churn.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:10.000
<v Priya>Five percent seems optimistic. We raised prices eighteen months ago and lost closer to nine.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:24.000
<v Sam>That was a bigger jump proportionally, and we didn't bundle anything with it. But you're right that it's a guess.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:40.000
<v Marcus>The thing that worries me is the customers who bought analytics separately last quarter. They'll effectively be paying twice until renewal.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:52.000
<v Sam>There are nineteen of those. We could credit them.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:09.000
<v Dana>Let's go with the sixty-five price and bundle analytics, and we'll credit the nineteen customers who bought the add-on separately. That's decided. Sam, can you write that up as a one-pager for the board by next Tuesday?

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:17.000
<v Sam>Will do.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:29.000
<v Dana>And someone needs to work out exactly what the credit looks like operationally.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:44.000
<v Sam>I'll take that too — I'll talk to Finance and come back with the mechanics.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:05:01.000
<v Priya>One open question: do we grandfather anyone who signed in the last thirty days? We don't know how many that is.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:12.000
<v Dana>Good question. Park that, we'll pick it up when Sam has the numbers.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:26.000
<v Dana>Last item, the support backlog. Marcus?

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:48.000
<v Marcus>It's at two hundred and forty tickets, up from one-ninety a fortnight ago. About a third of them are the same billing-export bug. If engineering fixes that, the backlog drops to something we can actually clear.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:06:03.000
<v Priya>That bug is on our board. It's not scheduled because it was P3, but if it's a third of the backlog it isn't a P3.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:15.000
<v Dana>Agreed, let's reprioritise it to P1.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:29.000
<v Priya>I'll move it and put it in this sprint. It should ship by Friday.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:47.000
<v Marcus>Thanks. I'll draft a note to the affected customers so it goes out the same day the fix lands.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:07:02.000
<v Dana>Perfect. Anything else? No? Then we're done — thanks everyone.
